        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Chose These Massage Guns</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these massage guns. They were chosen from published specifications,
            makers' product pages, manuals and warranty terms, recall records and independent
            retailer listings and reviews, and every claim on this page is limited to what those
            sources support.
          </p>
          <p>
            We started from sizes and budgets rather than brands: a full-size gun for people who
            train, a compact one for travel and a bag, a gentler gun for everyday aches, and a cheap
            one. For each, we looked for a model that its maker lists as on sale on 19 October 2026 and
            that is sold on Amazon US, and then checked each figure in two places. Rogue Fitness lists
            the Theragun Mini, the Theragun Relief and the Hypervolt 3 with full specifications, Titan
            Fitness and Recovery For Athletes list the Hypervolt 3 line, Torokhtiy lists the Ekrin B37,
            and Massage Gun Lab's review of the Theragun Prime gives its figures independently.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Safety screening.</strong> We searched the US Consumer Product Safety
            Commission's recall database by brand name for Therabody, Theragun, Hyperice, Hypervolt,
            Ekrin, RENPHO, Bob and Brad and TOLOCO, in recall titles, manufacturer names and recall
            descriptions, and found no recall for any of them. Searching by product type turned up
            three massager recalls: Massimo percussion massage guns sold at Costco, recalled in
            January 2021 for a fire hazard, and two HoMedics massager recalls, in May 2017 for
            electric shock and burn hazards and in January 2024 for fire and burn hazards. We did not
            consider either brand for this page. A massage gun is a lithium-ion battery pressed
            against your body, so buy from a seller who stands behind it, and see the charging
            advice in our safety section.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What we left out, and why.</strong> Bob and Brad's guns are popular on Amazon, but
            the amplitude and stall-force figures we found for its flagship model came only from
            third-party pages that disagreed with each other, and we could not confirm them from the
            maker on the day. TOLOCO and many similar marketplace brands sell guns whose specifications
            we could only find on the Amazon listing itself. Neither may be a bad gun; we simply could
            not check them. We also did not card Therabody's PRO Plus, which adds heat, light and
            vibration therapies for around $650, because nothing we could find independently shows
            those extras are worth that much more, or the outgoing Prime (5th Gen), which is still
            sold at clearance prices and is worth considering if you find it well under the
            6th Gen's price. Hyperice's smaller Hypervolt Go 3 ($149) and the six-speed Hypervolt 3 Pro
            ($349) are described below as alternatives.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="amplitude-stall-force" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Amplitude vs Stall Force vs Noise: Reading the Numbers</h2>
<p>
            Massage gun spec sheets lead with four numbers. Here is what each one measures, and how
            much weight to put on it.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Amplitude (stroke depth)</h3>
          <p>
            Amplitude is how far the head travels back and forth on each stroke, in millimetres.
            A longer stroke reaches further into a large muscle before the head pulls back; a shorter
            stroke feels lighter and more like a buzz. Therabody publishes amplitude clearly: 16 mm
            for its performance guns, including the Prime, 12 mm for the Sense and 10 mm for the
            Relief, which it describes as its gentlest. Hyperice does not list amplitude on its
            Hypervolt 3 product pages, and the figures quoted for those guns by third-party sites
            disagree with each other, so we have not printed one.
          </p>
          <p>
            In practice amplitude is the single most useful number for choosing between guns. If you
            want deep work on thighs and glutes, a 14 to 16 mm stroke is what people usually mean by
            "powerful". If the gun is mainly for shoulders, the upper back and general tension, a
            10 to 12 mm stroke is often more comfortable, not less effective for that job.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Stall force</h3>
          <p>
            Stall force is how hard you can press before the motor slows to a stop. It is the most
            quoted and least comparable number on the box. There is no shared test method, so one
            maker's 60 pounds is not necessarily another's. The figures also move between sources: for
            the Theragun Prime we found 30, 35 and 40 pounds quoted in different places, and
            Therabody's own product page lists none. Hyperice's launch announcement for the
            Hypervolt 3 gave 60 pounds, but its product page does not.
          </p>
          <p>
            More importantly, you should not be pressing anywhere near the stall force. Makers'
            instructions describe light pressure and letting the gun do the work. A higher stall
            force mostly matters to a therapist leaning a gun into someone else's thigh. For your own
            use, a gun that slows under the pressure you naturally apply is a reason to return it; a
            gun that claims 70 pounds rather than 50 is not a reason to buy it.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Speed (percussions per minute)</h3>
          <p>
            Speed is how many strokes the head makes per minute, given as PPM or RPM. Most guns run
            somewhere between about 1,400 and 3,200. Three well-spaced speeds are enough for most
            people; five or six give finer steps. A higher top speed is not more therapeutic. Many
            owners use the lowest or middle setting almost all the time.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Noise</h3>
          <p>
            Hyperice quotes 48 dB for the Hypervolt 3 and 55 dB for the smaller Go 3, RENPHO quotes up
            to 45 dB for the Active+, and a retailer lists the Ekrin B37 at 35 to 55 dB. Treat all of
            these as the makers' figures: none says at what distance, at which speed or with the head
            pressed on a muscle, and noise changes a lot with all three. What is consistent across
            reviews is that every gun gets louder at top speed and when pressed into a muscle, and that
            reviewers describe recent Theragun and Hypervolt models as quieter than early massage
            guns.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="attachments-battery-weight" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Attachments, Battery Life and Weight</h2>
<p>
            Once you have decided on a stroke depth, the practical details decide whether a gun gets
            used.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Attachments.</strong> You need fewer than the box suggests. A ball (sometimes
            called the standard or round head) is the general-purpose head; a soft, cushioned dampener
            is gentler and better near bony areas; a flat head spreads pressure over large muscles; and
            a thumb or bullet head concentrates it on one spot, which also makes it the easiest to
            overdo. A fork straddles a muscle and is sometimes sold for use alongside the spine. Two or
            three heads cover nearly everything. The Theragun Prime ships with two, the Mini and Relief
            with three, the Ekrin B37 with four and the Hypervolt 3 with five, including a heated head.
            Heat is a comfort feature; we found no independent evidence that it adds more than that.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Battery life.</strong> Makers rate battery life at the lowest speed, and Therabody
            says so in a footnote. The Prime is rated at 120 minutes, the Mini at up to 150 minutes on
            Therabody's US page (its own regional FAQ says around 120, depending on attachment and
            intensity), the Hypervolt 3 at up to four hours and the Ekrin B37 at up to eight. Because
            most sessions are a few minutes, even two hours is several weeks of normal use. Battery
            life matters most if you share a gun, travel without a charger or forget to charge
            things.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Charging.</strong> USB-C charging, as on the Prime, Mini, Relief and Hypervolt Go
            3, means one less proprietary charger to lose. The full-size Hypervolt 3 uses an 18-volt
            wall charger for the gun, which is less convenient but common on larger guns. Use the
            charger supplied or one the maker approves.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Weight and grip.</strong> Weight matters more than it looks on paper, because you
            hold the gun at arm's length, often behind you. The Hypervolt 3 and Theragun Prime are both
            about 2 pounds; the Relief is 1.37 pounds; the Mini is the smallest of the six. Therabody's
            triangular handle lets you change grip to reach the back of your own shoulder; Hyperice's
            pill-shaped, angled handle is simpler and suits a straight-armed grip. If you have small
            hands or a weak grip, lighter is better, and a compact gun you use is worth more than a
            full-size one that stays in the drawer.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Warranty and buying channel.</strong> Therabody's guns carry a one-year limited
            warranty. Ekrin offers a lifetime warranty, but its warranty page says that if you bought
            the gun anywhere other than Ekrin's own store — Amazon included — you must register it
            within 30 days of receipt or the cover is void. Do that the day it arrives. Therabody and
            Hyperice describe their guns as HSA and FSA eligible; whether your plan pays for one, and
            whether it wants a letter of medical necessity, depends on the plan, and our guide to
            <a href="/are-treadmills-hsa-fsa-eligible/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">HSA and FSA eligibility</a>
            explains how those rules generally work.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="evidence" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. What a Massage Gun Can and Cannot Do</h2>
<p>
            Massage guns are sold with confident claims. Therabody's product pages say its guns
            reduce delayed-onset muscle soreness, speed up recovery and increase flexibility, and cite
            company-run studies, such as a 12-week study of 104 subjects whose participants received
            compensation. Hyperice says its guns help loosen muscles, improve circulation and reduce
            soreness. On this page, those are the sellers' claims.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What the independent research shows so far.</strong> The evidence is real but
            thin. A 2023 systematic review in the International Journal of Sports Physical Therapy,
            which Therabody itself cites, found 13 studies of percussive therapy delivered by massage
            guns. The reviewers reported that every one of those studies had limitations in its
            methods or reporting. With that caveat, they found that a single session was associated
            with short-term increases in flexibility and in some strength measures, and that
            repeated treatments were associated with people reporting less muscle pain. The studies
            were small, short and used different guns, settings and timings, which makes firm
            conclusions difficult.
          </p>
          <p>
            Put plainly: a massage gun may help you feel less sore and move a little more freely for
            a while after using it, much as a foam roller or a massage can. There is no good evidence
            that it makes muscles repair faster, flushes anything out of them, prevents injury or
            makes you stronger over time. Research on foam rolling, a closely related practice, points
            the same way: small, short-lived effects on flexibility and perceived soreness, and
            little effect on performance.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What it does not replace.</strong> Sleep, eating enough, and a training plan that
            builds gradually and includes easier days do far more for how you feel between sessions
            than any device. If you are always sore or regularly in pain, the answer is rarely a
            stronger gun; it is the plan, the recovery around it, or a visit to a professional.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Massage gun or foam roller?</strong> They overlap. A gun is faster, can be used
            sitting down, and reaches the calves, forearms and front of the hips easily. A roller uses
            your bodyweight, covers more area per pass, needs no charging and costs far less. If you
            have neither, start with a roller; our
            <a href="/best-foam-rollers/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best foam rollers guide</a>
            covers firmness, texture and length. A gun makes most sense as an addition once you know
            you will use it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. How to Use a Massage Gun</h2>
<p>
            Read the manual that comes with your gun first; it is the authority on that device. These
            are general habits that match what the makers' instructions describe, not a treatment
            plan.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Start low and soft.</strong> Begin on the lowest speed with the softest
            attachment, usually a dampener or cushion head, and only move up if you want more.</li>
            <li><strong>Float, do not press.</strong> Rest the head on the muscle and let the gun's
            weight do most of the work. Hyperice's manual for the Hypervolt 2 Pro describes lightly
            pressing and moving across the body.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep moving and keep it short.</strong> The same manual suggests about 60
            seconds per region. Glide slowly along the muscle rather than holding the head on one
            spot.</li>
            <li><strong>Before training, a quick pass.</strong> A minute or two on the muscles you are
            about to use can be part of a warm-up, but follow it with lighter sets of the movement
            itself. Our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-warm-up-and-cool-down/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">warming up and cooling down</a>
            covers the general shape of both.</li>
            <li><strong>After training, slower.</strong> A few minutes on the legs or upper back
            after a session is how most people use a gun. If you lift and do cardio on the same day,
            <a href="/treadmill-before-or-after-weights/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether to do cardio before or after weights</a>
            matters more to how you feel than the gun does.</li>
            <li><strong>Mind hair, fingers and clothing.</strong> Hyperice warns to keep fingers,
            hair and other body parts away from the back of the head to avoid pinching or
            entanglement, and to keep the gun away from loose clothing and jewelry.</li>
            <li><strong>Give the gun a rest.</strong> Hyperice's manual says not to run its gun
            continuously for more than an hour and to let it rest for 30 minutes afterwards. You
            will rarely get near that, but sharing a gun at a busy home gym can.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Expect some tenderness the first few times, especially on the thumb head. Bruising is a
            sign of too much pressure or time; Hyperice's manual notes that bruising can occur at any
            pressure setting and that you should not use the gun on a bruise until it has fully
            healed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Where Not to Use a Massage Gun, and Who Should Ask First</h2>
<p>
            Both major makers publish lists of places and conditions where a massage gun should not be
            used, or should only be used after asking a doctor. Read the list for your exact model.
            What follows summarises what Therabody and Hyperice publish, as general guidance only.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Places to keep it away from</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>The front and sides of the neck, the throat, face and head.</strong>
            Therabody lists direct application to the face, head, throat, neck or genitalia among its
            contraindications, and direct pressure over the eyes or throat. Hyperice says its guns
            should be used only on soft tissue, not on hard or bony areas, including the head.</li>
            <li><strong>Bones, joints and bony points.</strong> The spine itself, the kneecap, the
            shin, the elbow and the collarbone. Therabody lists bony prominences as a precaution; use
            the gun on the muscle beside them instead.</li>
            <li><strong>Injuries and damaged skin.</strong> Bruises, rashes, open wounds, blisters,
            swelling, inflamed areas, a recent fracture, and any site of recent surgery or surgical
            hardware.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Ask a doctor or physiotherapist first if you</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>have, or have had, a blood clot such as deep vein thrombosis, or a history of
            embolism — Therabody lists both as contraindications;</li>
            <li>take blood-thinning medication, or have a bleeding disorder or a connective tissue
            disorder;</li>
            <li>are pregnant — Therabody lists pregnancy as a precaution requiring care and Hyperice
            asks you to get your physician's approval first;</li>
            <li>have uncontrolled high blood pressure, heart, liver or kidney disease, peripheral
            vascular disease or varicose veins;</li>
            <li>have diabetes with nerve damage, or any condition or medication that reduces or
            alters sensation, so you might not feel when it is too much;</li>
            <li>have a pacemaker or another implanted device, recent joint replacements, metal pins
            or plates, or a spinal condition such as a herniated disc;</li>
            <li>have osteoporosis or osteopenia, epilepsy or migraines, or a tumor, or are being
            treated for cancer.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            That is not a complete list, and it is not medical advice; it is a prompt to check. Stop
            immediately if the gun causes sharp pain, numbness, tingling or unusual swelling. Calf
            pain with swelling, warmth or redness, or sudden breathlessness, needs medical attention,
            not a massage gun.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Children and frail users.</strong> Hyperice says children and frail individuals
            should be supervised by an adult when using a percussion or vibration device. Keep guns
            out of reach of young children, who can trap fingers or hair in the moving head.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Battery safety.</strong> Charge with the supplied charger on a hard surface,
            where you can see it, and do not charge overnight or leave the gun unattended while it
            charges — Hyperice's manual says exactly that. Stop using any gun that gets unusually hot,
            swells, smells of burning or has been dropped hard and now behaves differently. Take it in
            your carry-on rather than checked luggage when flying, as Hyperice and Ekrin both advise for their guns.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Which Massage Gun Should You Buy?</h2>
<p>
            <strong>If you train several times a week</strong> and want one gun for legs, glutes and
            back, buy a full-size gun. The Theragun Prime has the deepest published stroke here and
            the most durable body; the Hypervolt 3 is a little cheaper, quieter by its maker's figure
            and comes with more heads. Either is a sensible choice, and the handle shape is a good
            tie-breaker.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If battery life or warranty matters most</strong>, the Ekrin B37 is the one to look
            at, particularly on sale. Register the warranty within 30 days if you buy it on Amazon.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you want a gun for your bag or for travel</strong>, the Theragun Mini is the
            established choice. Hyperice's Hypervolt Go 3 is the obvious alternative: Hyperice and
            Recovery For Athletes both list it at 1.6 pounds with five speeds and up to four hours of
            battery for $149, with two heads.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you are older, newer to massage or mainly want relief from everyday stiffness</strong>,
            a gentler gun such as the Theragun Relief is often more comfortable than a full-size
            one, and costs less.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you want to try a massage gun cheaply</strong>, RENPHO's Active+ is a reasonable
            way to find out whether you will use one, with the caveat that its figures are the maker's
            alone.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you have not bought any recovery kit yet</strong>, start with a foam roller and
            a lacrosse ball instead, and look at the gun again in a month. If you are still equipping
            the gym itself, our
            <a href="/home-workout-equipment-reviews/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home workout equipment reviews</a>
            cover what to buy first, and our
            <a href="/best-home-gym-accessories/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home gym accessories guide</a>
            covers the smaller kit that sits alongside it. If you box at home, the forearms and
            shoulders are where a gun earns its keep; our
            <a href="/best-boxing-equipment-for-home/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">boxing equipment guide</a>
            covers the rest of that setup.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best massage gun?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people who train, a full-size gun such as the Theragun Prime (6th Gen) or the Hyperice Hypervolt 3 is the best balance of depth, comfort and build. For travel, a compact gun like the Theragun Mini is easier to use regularly. For everyday stiffness, a gentler gun like the Theragun Relief is often more comfortable. The best one is the one you will actually use.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What matters more in a massage gun, amplitude or stall force?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Amplitude is the more useful number. It is how far the head travels on each stroke, and it decides how deep the massage feels. Stall force is how hard you can press before the motor stops, but makers measure it differently and you should be using light pressure anyway. Use amplitude to choose between guns, and treat stall force as a rough guide only.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do massage guns actually work?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 2023 systematic review of 13 studies found short-term improvements in flexibility and reduced reported muscle pain after massage gun use, but every study had limitations. A gun may help you feel looser and less sore for a while. There is no good evidence that it speeds healing, prevents injury or makes you stronger. Treat claims beyond that as the seller's.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where should you not use a massage gun?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Keep it off the front and sides of the neck, the throat, face and head, the spine itself and other bones and joints, and any bruise, wound, rash, swelling, fracture or recent surgery site. Ask a doctor first if you have had a blood clot, take blood thinners, are pregnant, have a heart or circulation condition, reduced sensation, an implanted device or another condition you are unsure about.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should you use a massage gun on one muscle?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Follow your gun's manual. Hyperice's manual suggests about 60 seconds per region, moving the head slowly over the muscle with light pressure rather than holding it in one spot. A whole session is usually a few minutes. If an area bruises or feels sore the next day, you used too much time or pressure there.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a massage gun better than a foam roller?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not better, different. A massage gun is quicker, can be used sitting down and reaches small areas easily. A foam roller covers more area, uses your bodyweight, needs no charging and costs much less. If you own neither, start with a foam roller and add a gun later if you want something faster and more targeted.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes When Buying and Using a Massage Gun</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most disappointments with massage guns come from buying on the box numbers or using the gun harder than it is meant to be used.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on stall force and top speed</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The biggest numbers on the box are the least comparable. Makers measure stall force differently, and a higher top speed is not more effective. Choose by amplitude, weight, handle and noise instead, and remember that most owners use the lower settings most of the time.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pressing hard or holding one spot</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A massage gun is meant to float over a muscle with light pressure. Leaning into it or holding the thumb head on one knot for minutes is how people end up bruised and sorer the next day. Keep it moving, keep each area to about a minute, and start on the softest head.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using it on the neck, bones or an injury</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The front and sides of the neck, the throat, the spine itself, kneecaps, shins and any bruised, swollen or injured area are off limits. Work the muscle beside a bone, not the bone. If something is injured, have it looked at rather than treating it with a gun.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the precautions list</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Makers list conditions where a massage gun should not be used without a doctor's approval, including blood clots, blood thinners, pregnancy and reduced sensation. It takes two minutes to read the list for your model, and it matters most for the people least likely to read it.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

